Sham-operated animals sit around 30 units, give or take about 8. And if we glance past this two-timepoint subset at the *entire* dataset (every DPI, every animal, the full course of the injury), GFAP intensity ranges from about 17 to 475. That single fact, "this assay has never produced a number outside roughly this envelope," is already enough to keep us from proposing something absurd.

::: callout-note
### This isn't the EDA chapter

I want to be explicit about the difference, because it matters: this is a five-second glance to keep a prior honest, not the deliberate, question-first exploration you'll see once we get to that part of the book. We're not looking for patterns, relationships, or anomalies here; just a sense of scale.
:::

## Specifying priors in `brms`

`brms` will tell you exactly what it's currently assuming, for every parameter in a model, if you ask it directly, before you fit anything:






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(brms)

# List every parameter of the model and the prior brms would use for it
get_prior(Gfap_Intensity ~ Condition, data = cell_data_sub)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
                    prior     class          coef group resp dpar nlpar lb ub
                   (flat)         b                                          
                   (flat)         b ConditionMCAO                            
 student_t(3, 98.7, 89.6) Intercept                                          
    student_t(3, 0, 89.6)     sigma                                      0   
       source
      default
 (vectorized)
      default
      default
```


:::
:::






Read that table like a witness statement, one row per parameter. The `ConditionMCAO` coefficient (our ischemia effect) gets `(flat)`: no opinion whatsoever, any real number equally credible, exactly the colleague we complained about a moment ago. `sigma`, the residual noise, gets a `student_t(3, 0, 89.6)`, structurally forced to stay positive regardless of what we specify (the parameter itself is declared non-negative under the hood, so even a prior family that technically extends below zero gets truncated there in practice). And notice the `Intercept`: `student_t(3, 98.7, 89.6)`. That's not flat. `brms` quietly centered it on roughly the mean GFAP value across the *whole* subset (sham and ischemic animals pooled together), which is a real, if unannounced, opinion. The lesson here isn't "brms's defaults are bad." It's that **there's no such thing as a genuinely opinion-free model.** Even the "flat" default made a choice about where to center the intercept; it just made that choice automatically, from the data, instead of asking you to make it deliberately from what you actually know.

So let's make our own choices, on purpose, using the numbers from the section above. `brms` takes a prior specification as a small vector built from `prior()` calls, one per parameter:






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# One prior() per parameter: sham baseline, group effect, residual noise
priors_v1 <- c(
  prior(normal(30, 20), class = "Intercept"),
  prior(normal(0, 150), class = "b", coef = "ConditionMCAO"),
  prior(normal(0, 30), class = "sigma")
)
```
:::






Here's the reasoning behind each line, because a prior without a stated reason is just a number you made up:

- **Intercept: `normal(30, 20)`.** Sham animals averaged about 30 in the table above, with a spread of roughly 8. A standard deviation of 20 gives real room around that (plausible sham intensities from close to zero up past 70) without pretending we have no idea at all where a sham animal's baseline sits.
- **`sigma`: `normal(0, 30)`, effectively half-normal thanks to that structural lower bound at zero.** Both groups' observed spreads (8 for sham, and the ischemic group's own spread in the table above) sit comfortably under 30, so most of this prior's mass covers noise levels at least as large as anything we've actually measured, with headroom for a noisier sample than this one.

The `ConditionMCAO` slope deserves its own paragraph, because there's a trap here worth naming honestly (@fig-priors_bullseye).

![Painting the bullseye around the arrow after it landed makes every shot look like a hit. A prior built from the answer you already saw does the same.](images/priors-regularization_bullseye-after-arrow.png){#fig-priors_bullseye fig-align="center" width="65%" fig-alt="Watercolor sketch of a researcher in a white lab coat standing on a wooden stepladder, painting concentric teal and ochre bullseye rings around an arrow already stuck in a weathered barn wall, with a small paint pot in one hand and a bow lying in the grass below."}

::: callout-important
### Why not just center the prior on the effect we already saw?

We already know, from the table above and from @sec-intro-applied-modeling, that the observed sham-vs-ischemia gap is around 107. The tempting move is to build a prior centered right there: `normal(107, 20)`, say. Don't. That's using the exact comparison you're about to test to construct the very belief you'll claim was independent of it. It's a [circular move]{style="color:red;"} that quietly stacks the deck before the model ever runs. A genuine prior reflects what you believed *before* this specific contrast, not a repackaging of the answer you already peeked at.

That's why the slope prior above is centered on **zero** ("before seeing this comparison, I don't assume ischemia raises or lowers GFAP"), with its width, 150, grounded instead in the *broader*, comparison-independent fact from the section above: this assay has never produced a swing bigger than roughly the full 17–475 range. That's legitimate prior knowledge: a fact about the measurement itself, not a repackaging of the specific answer we're about to test.
:::

::: callout-tip
### A trap for later: priors on a link scale

Every prior in this chapter lives on the same scale as GFAP intensity itself, because our model uses an identity link: the coefficients *are* GFAP units, so "does this number look plausible" is a direct question. That stops being true the moment we move to a model with a non-identity link, like the generalized linear models waiting for us in [the introduction to GLMs](glm-intro.qmd#sec-glm-intro). A prior that *looks* just as timid there, say `normal(0, 100)` on a log-odds coefficient, is actually one of the most opinionated priors you could write: the logistic function saturates, so that "vague-looking" number dumps almost all its prior mass onto probabilities crushed up against 0 or 1. Keep this in your back pocket; we'll return to it properly once we're there.
:::

## Before touching real data: the prior predictive check

Here's an idea we've already used once, back when we built a fake dataset from chosen parameters to check whether our fitting machinery could recover a known truth. A prior is a claim about what data *could* plausibly look like, even before any real data exists, which means we can run it forward, exactly like we ran that generative model forward, and just look at what it produces. That's called a [prior predictive check]{style="color:green; font-weight:bold;"}, and it's just as cheap to run as the check we already did: if `brms` can sample the posterior by exploring the space of parameter values, it can just as easily sample from the *prior alone*, by setting `sample_prior = "only"` and simply not letting the likelihood weigh in:






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# sample_prior = "only" ignores the likelihood: the draws come from the priors alone
prior_check_fit <- brm(
  Gfap_Intensity ~ Condition,
  data = cell_data_sub,
  prior = priors_v1,
  sample_prior = "only",
  seed = 123,
  file = "models/priors-regularization_fit1.rds",
  file_refit = "never"
)

# 100 simulated datasets (faint curves) against the observed data (dark curve);
# the legend sits in the empty upper right of the panel
pp_check(prior_check_fit, ndraws = 100) +
  labs(x = "GFAP intensity (simulated from priors only)", y = "Density") +
  Plot_theme +
  legend_inside(0.88, 0.8)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Prior predictive check: simulated GFAP values from the priors alone, before seeing any real data](priors-regularization_files/figure-html/fig-priors_prior_predictive-1.png){#fig-priors_prior_predictive width=576}
:::
:::






Every one of those faint curves in @fig-priors_prior_predictive is a full fake dataset, generated purely from our priors, before a single real animal's measurement was involved. This is the positive control from last chapter, run one step earlier in the pipeline: instead of asking "can the model recover a truth I planted," we're asking "does the model, armed with nothing but these priors, even propose scientifically sane data." The figure comes from `pp_check()` in `brms`, which overlays each simulated dataset as a faint curve on the observed one (the dark curve); we use it because it needs no plotting code of our own and, as the first thing you will do with any new model, it is worth knowing by name. @fig-priors_fake_datasets draws the same idea as a stack of tracing-paper sheets.

![Before a single real animal is measured, the priors already imagine a hundred datasets. Most look sensible; a few dip below the floor where no staining intensity can go.](images/priors-regularization_hundred-fake-datasets.png){#fig-priors_fake_datasets fig-align="center" width="90%" fig-alt="Watercolor sketch of a fan of translucent tracing-paper sheets on a bench, each with a different faint bell-shaped curve in teal or ochre overlapping into a cloud. A dotted ground line crosses every sheet, and the left tails of some curves dip below it in rust-orange. A pencil and a plain ruler rest beside the sheets."}













It mostly does, but not entirely. About 36% of the simulated values in that cloud are negative, which is a physical impossibility for a staining intensity. That's not a disaster, and I'm not going to pretend it is just to manufacture drama: our priors are still [weakly informative]{style="color:green; font-weight:bold;"} (opinionated enough to rule out the truly implausible, loose enough to leave everything else on the table) rather than tightly constrained, and a Gaussian model has no way to know on its own that its outcome can't cross zero. Catching that honestly, in a chapter about priors, is exactly the point: we've made the model noticeably more scientifically literate than the flat default, without pretending it's now perfect. A model that can still occasionally imagine negative protein intensity is a model we'll want to check more rigorously against real predictions once we have the tools for it, which is precisely what @sec-mcmc-model-checking, on posterior predictive checks, is for.

## Refitting the ischemia model with real priors

With the prior predictive check giving us reasonable confidence that we're not handing `brms` something scientifically silly, let's put these priors to work on the real data (the same model, same fifteen animals, as in @sec-intro-applied-modeling and @sec-bayesian-core-workflow):






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# The same model as before, now with the priors we specified (fit is cached)
informed_fit <- brm(
  Gfap_Intensity ~ Condition,
  data = cell_data_sub,
  prior = priors_v1,
  seed = 123,
  file = "models/priors-regularization_fit2.rds",
  file_refit = "never"
)

summary(informed_fit)
```

