Rhat tops out at 1.00 and both effective sample sizes clear 400 comfortably across every parameter, so this fit has earned our trust the same way last chapter's did.

Now the question we actually care about: did any of this matter? Let's put the prior itself, the original flat-prior posterior from @sec-intro-applied-modeling, and this new informed posterior on the same axis and look (@fig-priors_prior_posterior_overlay). Densities on a shared axis are the honest way to compare distributions; three separate summary numbers would hide whether the curves overlap. Colour, line type and line weight all carry the same information, so the figure still reads in grayscale:






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Reload the flat-prior fit from the first chapter (cached, not refitted)
flat_fit <- brm(
  Gfap_Intensity ~ Condition,
  data = cell_data_sub,
  seed = 123,
  file = "models/intro-applied-modeling_fit1.rds",
  file_refit = "never"
)

curve_levels <- c("Prior", "Posterior, flat prior", "Posterior, informed prior")

# Stack the draws of the group effect from the three sources into one table
overlay_df <- data.frame(
  beta1 = c(
    as_draws_df(prior_check_fit)$b_ConditionMCAO,
    as_draws_df(flat_fit)$b_ConditionMCAO,
    as_draws_df(informed_fit)$b_ConditionMCAO
  ),
  curve = factor(rep(curve_levels, each = 4000), levels = curve_levels)
)

# Three densities on one axis; colour, line type and weight all encode the source,
# so the curves stay distinguishable in grayscale and where they overlap
ggplot(overlay_df, aes(x = beta1, color = curve, fill = curve, linetype = curve, linewidth = curve)) +
  geom_density(alpha = 0.3) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("grey45", "#0072B2", "#D55E00"), name = NULL) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("grey70", "#0072B2", "#D55E00"), name = NULL) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("solid", "solid", "42"), name = NULL) +
  scale_linewidth_manual(values = c(0.5, 1, 1), name = NULL) +
  coord_cartesian(xlim = c(-300, 500)) +
  labs(x = "Ischemia effect on GFAP intensity", y = "Density") +
  Plot_theme +
  # Legend in the empty upper right of the panel
  legend_inside(0.81, 0.8) +
  theme(legend.key.width = unit(1.4, "lines"), legend.text = element_text(size = 9))
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Prior, flat-prior posterior, and informed-prior posterior for the ischemia effect](priors-regularization_files/figure-html/fig-priors_prior_posterior_overlay-1.png){#fig-priors_prior_posterior_overlay width=624}
:::
:::






The pale, wide curve is the prior, spread thin across a huge range, exactly as intended. The other two curves, the flat-prior and informed-prior posteriors, sit almost on top of each other, both tall and narrow, both centered right around 107. With fifteen animals showing a gap this clean, the data is already carrying the argument almost entirely on its own; our prior had an opinion, but the evidence simply outvoted it. That's not a failure of the exercise; it's exactly what should happen when you have enough good data. A prior's job is to matter *when the data can't settle things by itself*, and right now, it can.

::: {.callout-note title="How to report it"}
**Methods.** We used weakly informative priors, stated before fitting: a normal prior with mean 30 and standard deviation 20 for the sham baseline (from the sham mean of 30 and its spread), a normal prior centered on zero with standard deviation 150 for the ischemia effect (a width taken from the full range of the assay, 17 to 475, not from the contrast under test), and a half-normal prior with scale 30 for the residual standard deviation. A prior predictive check showed that 36% of simulated values were negative; we judged this acceptable for weakly informative priors and examined the fit with posterior predictive checks.

**Results.** With these priors, GFAP intensity three days after ischemia was 107.3 units higher than in sham animals (95% credible interval 83.3 to 130), essentially the same as with the default flat prior, so the conclusion does not depend on the prior.
:::

::: callout-tip
### Frequentist equivalent

Nothing changes here on the frequentist side, and for the same reason @sec-intro-applied-modeling flagged already: with this much data pointing this clearly in one direction, `lm()` and an informed `brms` fit are converging on the same answer as the flat-prior fit did. Where they'll actually part ways is coming up next.
:::

## Where a prior earns its keep: small samples

A prior that agrees with fifteen well-behaved animals isn't proving very much. The honest test is a dataset too small to speak clearly on its own, which is exactly the generative-model exercise from last chapter, run again with far fewer animals:






::: {#tbl-priors_sim_small .cell tbl-cap='A deliberately tiny simulated dataset (same planted truth as last chapter\'s simulation)'}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Fixed seed so the simulated dataset is identical on every render
set.seed(2025)

# The truth we plant: baseline, group effect and noise (as in the previous chapter)
true_beta0 <- 30
true_beta1 <- 107
true_sigma <- 20
n_small <- 3

# Three SHAM and three MCAO animals
sim_small <- data.frame(
  Condition = factor(rep(c("SHAM", "MCAO"), each = n_small), levels = c("SHAM", "MCAO"))
)
# Generate GFAP values from the model equation plus random noise
sim_small$Gfap_Intensity <- true_beta0 +
  true_beta1 * (sim_small$Condition == "MCAO") +
  rnorm(nrow(sim_small), mean = 0, sd = true_sigma)

gt(sim_small) |> fmt_number(columns = Gfap_Intensity, decimals = 1)
```

