:::
:::








By sheer luck of the draw, the point estimate (107.3) actually landed close to the truth this time; small samples don't always land this well, which is itself worth remembering. Now let's fit the same tiny dataset in `brms`, first with no prior stated at all (the same flat default from @sec-intro-applied-modeling), so we can isolate what *being Bayesian* buys us before we ask what an *informative prior* buys us:






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# brms with its default (flat) slope prior on the tiny dataset
sim_small_flat <- brm(
  Gfap_Intensity ~ Condition,
  data = sim_small,
  seed = 123,
  file = "models/priors-regularization_fit3.rds",
  file_refit = "never"
)
```
:::








This fit passes the same checks as before (Rhat under 1.01, both effective sample sizes above 400). Even with a flat prior on the slope, the Bayesian credible interval (width 62) comes out wider than the frequentist confidence interval (width 53). That's not the slope prior doing anything; it's a small-sample effect on the noise. `lm()` does account for not knowing `sigma`: its interval uses a $t$ distribution with only four residual degrees of freedom, and under a flat reference prior on the coefficients and on $\log \sigma$ the Bayesian posterior for the effect is exactly that same $t$ distribution [@gelman2020; @gelman2024]. But `brms` does not use a reference prior. It keeps its default weakly informative priors on the intercept and on `sigma`, and with only three animals per group there is too little data to overrule them: they leave more plausible room for a large `sigma`, and a larger `sigma` means a wider interval for the effect. The gap is temporary: as the sample grows, the likelihood swamps those priors, the posterior for `sigma` tightens around the residual standard error, and the two intervals converge. With six data points, we genuinely don't know `sigma` very well, and this is what that looks like.

Now let's actually give the prior something to do. Rather than fit several near-identical models by hand, let's dial the width of our belief about the slope (keeping the same `normal(0, ...)` shape, centered on "no assumed direction," from wide to narrow) and watch what happens to both the estimate and the interval (@tbl-priors_shrinkage_sweep, then @fig-priors_shrinkage). The figure uses `geom_pointrange()` from `ggplot2`: one estimate with its interval per row, and the planted truth as a dashed line, so you judge each fit against the answer instead of against the others. A bar with an error whisker could not show a dashed truth line this way, and would have started at zero, which means nothing for a difference.






::: {#tbl-priors_shrinkage_sweep .cell tbl-cap='How the ischemia-effect estimate moves as the slope prior tightens'}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Three widths for the slope prior, from wide to narrow, one cached fit each
prior_widths <- c(150, 60, 25)
sweep_files <- paste0("models/priors-regularization_fit", 4:6, ".rds")

# Refit the tiny dataset once per prior width, changing only the slope prior
sweep_fits <- Map(function(sd_b, file) {
  p <- c(
    prior(normal(30, 20), class = "Intercept"),
    set_prior(paste0("normal(0,", sd_b, ")"), class = "b", coef = "ConditionMCAO"),
    prior(normal(0, 30), class = "sigma")
  )
  brm(
    Gfap_Intensity ~ Condition, data = sim_small, prior = p, seed = 123,
    file = file, file_refit = "never"
  )
}, prior_widths, sweep_files)

# For each fit: median, 95% interval, interval width and sampler checks
sweep_summary <- do.call(rbind, lapply(seq_along(sweep_fits), function(i) {
  draws <- as_draws_df(sweep_fits[[i]])
  b <- draws$b_ConditionMCAO
  ci <- quantile(b, c(0.025, 0.975))
  diag <- summarise_draws(draws, "rhat", "ess_bulk")
  diag <- subset(diag, variable %in% c("b_Intercept", "b_ConditionMCAO", "sigma"))
  data.frame(
    prior_sd = prior_widths[i],
    median = median(b),
    ci_low = ci[1],
    ci_high = ci[2],
    width = diff(ci),
    max_rhat = max(diag$rhat),
    min_ess = min(diag$ess_bulk)
  )
}))

# Stop the render if any of the three fits has a sampler problem
stopifnot(max(sweep_summary$max_rhat) < 1.01, min(sweep_summary$min_ess) > 400)

gt(sweep_summary) |> fmt_number(columns = -prior_sd, decimals = 1)
```

